\par\par \b \uc2  EMERGING MARKETS-Romania on a knife-edge. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Gill Tudor \par \uc2 584 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1999\par 19:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, March 19 (Reuters) - Romania is on a knife-edge, caught up in an ethical argument between foreign banks and the International Monetary Fund that could cost it crucial external funding and plunge it into further economic turmoil. \par \par \uc2  Overseas analysts said the eastern European country desperately needed foreign finance to stave off debt default and economic collapse, but an IMF diktat that banks must shoulder their share of the burden had put that support at risk. \par \par \uc2  "You've got private finance saying it doesn't want to lend money, and the IMF saying 'If you don't want to lend money, why should we?'" said Charles Robertson, emerging Europe economist at ING Barings in London. "The Romanian authorities are stuck in the middle." \par \par \uc2  A current account deficit of around seven percent of gross domestic product combined with a spike in debt service obligations mean Romania must bridge an external financing gap of at least $5 billion this year, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Maturing debt amounts to $2.8-3.0 billion, of which about $1.0 billion falls due in May and June alone. \par \par \uc2  Lined up against that, Romania has foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of about $1.5 billion and gold reserves worth $900 million. \par \par \uc2  A privatisation programme is under way, but deals so far on companies such as telecom operator Romtelecom or oil refinery Petromidia have brought in little hard cash after debt write-offs and investment obligations are taken into account. \par \par \uc2  The country is seeking funding of around $1 billion from the World Bank and IMF this year, with approval needed from both institutions to unlock either loan. These would then pave the way for other multilateral funding. \par \par \uc2  But the IMF has told Romania it should refinance or roll over some $800 million in principal and interest coming due this year on two Eurobonds and a yen Samurai bond, arguing that it cannot be expected continually to bail out private investors. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Romania was determined not to default on the bonds, and bankers said it had made no approach to that end. \par \par \uc2  But foreign reserves are already under siege from the sliding leu \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , currently bid on the interbank market at around 15,000 to the dollar against 11,000 at the start of 1999. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Thursday it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when and where needed. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Romania\b0 \highlight\uc2  is in real trouble," said Nick Douch, emerging markets analyst at Barclays Capital. "It's a classic case where if they continually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  they worsen the credit risk." \par \par \uc2  Analysts said \b \highlight22\uc2 Romania\b0 \highlight\uc2  needed a weaker leu to ease its current account problems, but a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  freefall would spook investors and Romanians alike and could topple the fragile coalition government even before general elections next year. \par \par \uc2  One possible way through the May-June debt crunch is for Romania either to sell some gold reserves or - more probably - to use the gold as collateral for fresh commercial borrowing. \par \par \uc2  But Robertson at ING Barings said the IMF was likely to object to such an arrangement, arguing it would effectively run down Romania's reserves while insulating the banks from risk. \par \par \uc2  To gain IMF support the centre-right government must also press on with sweeping structural reforms, but the social pain that these inevitably cause is eroding its popular support. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said former communist and nationalist parties were waiting in the wings and could form a new government hostile to foreign investors. (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dv3j025pw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
